<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="Cairns hike planner" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1">
  <metadata>
    <name>West Tiger 3</name>
    <desc>High Point trailhead to the West Tiger 3 summit and back. 8.8 km, +607 m, −605 m.</desc>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="47.529558" lon="-121.995885">
    <ele>162.3</ele>
    <name>1 High Point trailhead</name>
    <desc>Discover Pass on the dashboard. Toilet at the trailhead, no water: carry 1–1.5 L each. The road gate is open dawn to dusk; if the lot is full, park outside the gate. Walk past the kiosk and take the West Tiger 3 Trail: the Bus Road and Tiger Mountain Trail cross it in the first 300 m.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.521720" lon="-121.990972">
    <ele>346.7</ele>
    <name>2 Talus Rocks junction</name>
    <desc>The Talus Rocks trail goes straight (a side trip to boulder caves); turn left and keep climbing on the West Tiger 3 Trail. From here it is steady, wide and rocky, about 15%, switchback after switchback.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.516910" lon="-121.986585">
    <ele>602.3</ele>
    <name>3 West Tiger Railroad Grade</name>
    <desc>The old railroad grade crosses here, 600 m up. Eat now: the last 170 m of climb are the steepest of the hike.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.515200" lon="-121.987208">
    <ele>682.4</ele>
    <name>4 Cable Line junction</name>
    <desc>The Cable Line Trail joins here: it runs straight down the fall line to the north, steep and muddy. Remember this junction for the way down. The trees thin out above here; put a layer on before the top.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.511914" lon="-121.990146">
    <ele>769.4</ele>
    <name>5 West Tiger 3 summit</name>
    <desc>An open, logged summit with benches of rock: Issaquah, Lake Sammamish, Seattle and the Olympics to the west, Rainier to the south on a clear day. Often in the clouds when the valley is clear, and busy even midweek.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.515200" lon="-121.987208">
    <ele>682.4</ele>
    <name>6 Cable Line junction (down)</name>
    <desc>Stay on the wide West Tiger 3 Trail. The Cable Line branching off here looks like a shortcut: it is a steep, muddy fall-line trail that comes out on the access road about 600 m east of the lot.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.516910" lon="-121.986585">
    <ele>602.3</ele>
    <name>7 Railroad Grade (down)</name>
    <desc>Straight across the railroad grade and on down. Loose rock on the steeper pitches: short steps, poles if you have them.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.521720" lon="-121.990972">
    <ele>346.7</ele>
    <name>8 Talus Rocks junction (down)</name>
    <desc>Keep right (downhill) on the West Tiger 3 Trail. The last 1.5 km is gentler forest, crossing the Tiger Mountain Trail and the Bus Road just before the lot.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.529558" lon="-121.995885">
    <ele>162.3</ele>
    <name>9 High Point trailhead</name>
    <desc>Finish. Back at the car.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>West Tiger 3</name>
